 "dot_count_effect": {
  "spearman_n_vs_score": -0.9666666666666667,
  "ols_slope_per_1000_dots": -0.00119,
  "interpretation": "Within the nine-file family the score tracks dot count almost perfectly and nothing else does; the family is one near-uniform covering of the same support (median mutual 3-px overlap 95.4 %), so its dots are statistically interchangeable and n is the only free parameter that moves the score."
 },
 "conclusion": {
  "corrections_2026_10_31": "The identity was corrected (coefficient on T is 1-beta = 0.2, not 1). Consequences: perfect-knowledge ceiling = 1.0 (was 0.556); marginal bar at 0.2778 = 0.0588 (was 0.0769); bar at 0.4413 = 0.0968 (was 0.158); the coverage needed for 0.4413 at B2's budget is about 1.6x the nine files' coverage (was 1.86x per-dot). Checked against gems57.metric.dti_bruteforce.",
  "why_0.2778": "Measured, not narrated. Among the nine, score is monotone in dot count (Spearman -0.97). GEMSDOE32's B2 is an exact subset of GEMSDOE25 (37,654 of 44,090 dots; 6,436 removed, none added; verified 31 Oct 2026 from the registry rasters). Removing those dots raised the score from 0.2600 to 0.2778 on the same truth, so in aggregate the removed dots earned less truth credit than the marginal bar (0.0588 at s = 0.2778). 0.2778 is the best point of that one-parameter family, not a different geological idea.",
  "can_it_be_beaten": "Yes, but only by raising truth credit per dot. A dot raises DTI only if its kernel credit exceeds the marginal bar (0.0588 at 0.2778, 0.0968 at 0.4413). At B2's budget with G = 60,988 px (assumed), 0.2778 corresponds to about 0.26 truth coverage; the nine files cover about 0.25-0.26. A candidate with the same dot count needs a clearly higher hit rate per dot to move the score, and this session did not produce one on a valid instrument.",
  "is_0.4413_reachable": "At B2's budget and G = 60,988 px (assumed), 0.4413 needs about 0.41 truth coverage, roughly 1.6x the nine files at the same dot count, with a marginal-dot credit bar of 0.0968. Whether that is geometrically hard depends on the unknown size of the private truth. Nothing in this repository has demonstrated that level on a valid instrument; the best local evidence (SGMC off-catalogue, leave-one-quadrant-out) is far below it and is anti-correlated with the observed ordering. The honest statement is that 0.4413 exists on the leaderboard and is not reachable from the evidence this repository holds."
 }
}
